## Scope

What this sub-phase delivers, in a few bullets. One focused area: if it spans unrelated concerns, split it.

## Out of scope

What it deliberately leaves out, and which later sub-phase (or "later") picks it up.

{{#if hasDatabase}}
## Schema changes

New or changed tables, columns, indexes and constraints{{#if isDrizzle}} (Drizzle schema in `db/schema.ts`, with the migration generated and reviewed){{/if}}{{#if isConvex}} (`convex/schema.ts`){{/if}}. How existing data moves.

{{/if}}
## Modules and functions

The services and modules added or changed, and what each owns. Name the registry a new member joins.

## Agent surface

*Required when the spec adds or changes a user capability.* The service function and every surface that exposes it ({{#if isORPC}}its oRPC procedure and REST route{{/if}}{{#if isTRPC}}its tRPC procedure{{/if}}{{#unless hasApi}}its server functions or route handlers{{/unless}}{{#if isMcp}}, and its MCP tool{{else}}, and an MCP tool once the app has one{{/if}}), with its auth scope and rate limit; or why it isn't exposed yet. Agents are first-class users: a capability people have, agents get too.

## Cost and latency budget

*Required when the spec adds paid calls (LLM, email, payments, third-party APIs) or a user-facing run.* Calls per billable unit, cost per unit, margin at full plan use, p50/p95 latency targets, and cache and reuse assumptions. Every paid call goes through one layer that records its cost.

{{#if hasDatabase}}
## Query budget

*Required when a read path is added or changed.* Queries per request or page, no N+1, the indexes used, and `EXPLAIN` for hot paths.

{{/if}}
## Copy

*Required for user-facing specs.* Headings, captions, empty states, errors and button labels, written out: plain words, one clear next action, American English.

## Registries

A new member of a concept goes in its registry. A new concept that spans layers names its registry, or says why not yet.

## Testing plan

The invariants that matter, each named (I1, I2, …), and the test that would fail if it broke. Fixtures shaped like real data.

## Verification

How to see it working: commands to run, pages to open, what to look for.

## Risks

What could go wrong, and how we'd notice.

## Exit checklist

- [ ] Every scope item built
- [ ] Every invariant has a test
- [ ] `{{{pm.run}}} check` is green
{{#if isGated}}
- [ ] Reviews run and recorded in `docs/reviews/`; findings fixed or deferred with a trigger
- [ ] Budgets measured and met, or the breach accepted in writing
{{/if}}
- [ ] Docs updated ({{docFile}}, guides, `.env.example`)
- [ ] `SPEC_MASTER_CHECKLIST.md` ticked; ADRs recorded

## Notes from the build

Surprises, decisions made on the way (with their ADRs), and what the next sub-phase should know.
